== 10.1  Definitions: perfectly balanced trees
OK   perfectly balanced tree on n keys: Imbal <= 1 everywhere, height floor(lg n) edges = ceil(lg(n+1)) levels
OK   over all BST shapes with n <= 9 keys: minimum height floor(lg n) (= ceil(lg(n+1)) levels), maximum n - 1
== 10.2  Lazy deletions and global rebuilding
OK   global rebuilding: fewer than 2n nodes in the tree, so a search visits at most ceil(lg n) + 1 nodes (n = unmarked keys)
OK   global rebuilding: searches report marked keys as absent and unmarked keys as present
OK   global rebuilding: each rebuild builds at most (#deletions since the last one) nodes and traverses at most twice that many; total rebuild work (old tree + new tree) <= 3 x deletions (O(1) amortized)
OK   the note's bound 'at most lg n + 1 keys' fails: 5-node tree with 2 marked (n = 3) has a 3-node search path > lg 3 + 1 = 2.58  [(208, 127, 8)]
== 10.3  Partial rebuilding (insertions)
OK   partial rebuilding (180 runs: alpha in [1.1, 1.25, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0], two size/height conventions, n up to 1500, random and adversarial orders): result is a BST of the inserted keys
OK   after every insertion no node violates height(v) <= alpha lg size(v), and stored sizes/heights are right
OK   after every insertion the tree's height is at most alpha lg n (n keys; with null leaves, alpha lg(2n+1))
OK   at most one subtree is rebuilt per insertion (rebuilding the lowest violator fixes all its ancestors)
OK   proof of Lemma 1 at every rebuild: height(v) = height(side) + 1, height(side) <= alpha lg size(side), size(side) > size(v)/2^(1/alpha), size(other) < (1 - 2^(-1/alpha)) size(v) - 1
OK   the note's last line: Imbal(v) >= size(side) - size(other) - 1 > (2/2^(1/alpha) - 1) size(v)
OK   Lemma 1: Imbal(v) > c size(v) with c = 2^(1-1/alpha) - 1 > 0 (and even size(side) - size(other) > c size(v) + 1)
OK   charging: at a rebuild, the insertions into v's subtree since it was built are >= Imbal(v) - 1 > c size(v)
OK   total rebuild work <= (1/c) x (sum over insertions of the number of ancestors of the new node)
OK   the constant c = 2^(1-1/alpha) - 1: 0.26 for alpha = 3/2, 0.41 for alpha = 2
== 10.4  Scapegoat trees: both kinds of rebuilding
OK   scapegoat tree (random mixes of 3000 insertions and deletions, alpha = 1.25, 1.5, 2): membership correct
OK   scapegoat tree: worst-case search visits at most alpha lg N + 1 nodes, N < 2n nodes in all (marked included)
OK   scapegoat tree: at most one partial rebuild per insertion; total cost within the amortized O(log n) bounds
OK   O(1)-space variant: sizes along a path computed bottom-up from sibling subtrees visit exactly size(top) nodes
== 10.5–10.6  Rotations, splaying, splay trees
OK   a rotation keeps the in-order sequence; x moves up one level, its old parent down one
OK   zig-zag: x up 2, parent unchanged, grandparent down 1; roller-coaster: x up 2, parent unchanged, grandparent down 2
OK   splaying x performs exactly depth(x) rotations and makes x the root
OK   Figure 4's tree is a BST for the order a < ... < j < x < k < ... < n
OK   Figure 4 replayed: the four intermediate trees are exactly the note's panels  [['b(a,d(c,m(k(f(e,h(g,j(i,x))),l),n)))', 'b(a,d(c,m(k(f(e,x(j(h(g,i),-),-)),l),n)))', 'b(a,d(c,m(x(f(e,j(h(g,i),-)),k(-,l)),n)))', 'b(a,x(d(c,f(e,j(h(g,i),-))),m(k(-,l),n)))', 'x(b(a,d(c,f(e,j(h(g,i),-)))),m(k(-,l),n))']]
OK   Figure 4: x at depth 7; roller-coaster, zig-zag, zig-zag, single rotation: 7 rotations
OK   Figure 4: depths of the old path b, d, m, k, f, h, j go from 0,1,2,3,4,5,6 to 1,2,1,2,3,5,4
OK   Figure 4: Phi (sum of lg size) drops from 20.95 to 19.40; amortized cost 5.45 <= 1 + 3 lg 15 = 12.72
OK   splay tree Search/Insert/Delete agree with a set on 132 random and adversarial sequences; BST, parents, sizes valid
OK   Access Lemma (unit weights) at every step: single <= 1 + 3(r'(x) - r(x)), double <= 3(r'(x) - r(x)) (55770 single, 122055 zig-zag, 147091 roller-coaster steps)
OK   every line of the corrected proof holds at every step (incl. r(y) >= r(x), r'(y) <= r(z), r'(x) = r(z), s(x) + s'(z) <= s'(x), s'(w) + s'(z) <= s'(x))
OK   per operation: Search <= 1 + 3 lg n, Insert <= 1 + 4 lg n (attaching a leaf raises Phi by <= lg n), Delete <= 2 + 6 lg n (the join raises Phi by <= lg n)
OK   total rotations = total amortized cost - Phi_end (Phi_0 = 0), hence <= total amortized cost
OK   p. 7, zig-zag, 2nd line as printed (2 + r'(w) + r'(x) + r'(z) - 2r(x)) is not equal to the 3rd line (it has an extra r'(x)); the corrected 2nd line 2 + r'(w) + r'(z) - 2r(x) is  [122055 steps differ]
OK   p. 7, roller-coaster bracket 'r(x) >= r(y)' is backwards (x is y's child before the step, so r(x) < r(y)): false at every roller-coaster step, while r(y) >= r(x) always holds  [147091 of 147091 steps]
OK   only x and its parent (and grandparent) change rank in a step: Delta Phi over all nodes = the local change
OK   whole splay: amortized cost <= 1 + 3(r(root) - r(x)) <= 1 + 3 lg n (2000 random trees)
OK   the note's '3 lg n - 1' is not what the telescoping gives: a one-node tree splays at amortized cost 0 > 3 lg 1 - 1 = -1; the bound is 1 + 3 lg n
OK   perfectly balanced trees have Phi = Theta(n) and paths Theta(n log n), with lg or floor(lg) ranks  [balanced Phi/n -> 1.364; path Phi/(n lg n) -> 0.910]
OK   floor ranks: a roller-coaster with sizes (x, y, z) = (8, 9, 13) -> (13, 8, 4) changes sum floor(lg size) by -1 only, so its amortized cost 2 - 1 = 1 exceeds 3(floor lg 13 - floor lg 8) = 0
OK   floor ranks: even a whole splay can exceed 1 + 3(floor lg n - rank(x)) (counterexample found by search)  [(54, 3, 5, 4)]
== 10.7  Weighted ranks: generalized Access Lemma, static optimality, static finger
OK   generalized Access Lemma with random positive weights (e^-5 .. e^5): every step and every proof line
OK   generalized: splaying x costs <= 1 + 3(r(root) - r(x)) amortized
OK   weights must be positive: a zero-weight leaf has size 0 and rank lg 0 = -infinity (the note says non-negative)
OK   static optimality: with w(x) = t(x), each access of x costs <= 1 + 3 lg(T/t(x)) amortized
OK   static optimality: total rotations <= sum of those bounds + sum_x lg(T/t(x)) (the most Phi can drop)
OK   static finger: with w(x) = 1/dist(f,x)^2 the total weight is <= pi^2/3 for every n and finger
OK   static finger: each access costs <= 1 + 3(lg(pi^2/3) + 2 lg dist(f,x)) = O(1 + log dist) amortized
OK   scanning theorem, empirically: splaying all nodes in order takes < 4.5 n rotations (random trees, paths)  [max 4.36 n]
== Concept page example: a path of 15, then Search(1)
OK   inserting 1..15 in increasing order costs 1 rotation each after the first (14) and leaves a left path
OK   Search(1) on that path: 14 rotations, height 14 -> 8, Phi = lg 15! = 40.25 -> 23.21, amortized -3.04 <= 1 + 3 lg 15 = 12.72
OK   after inserting 1..n in increasing order, searching for the smallest key takes n - 1 rotations
OK   move-to-root (single rotations) on a path: accessing all keys in increasing order costs n(n+1)/2 - 1 rotations and restores the path, round after round (about n/2 per access)
